# flowspec-malformed-nlri-reset -- RFC 8955 Section 4.2, a received FlowSpec NLRI that
# is not encoded as specified resets the session, over a live iBGP session.
#
# Section 4.2: "An NLRI value not encoded as specified here, including an NLRI that
# contains an unknown component type, is considered malformed and error handling according
# to Section 10 is performed." Section 10 defers to RFC 7606, whose Section 3 (j) allows
# treat-as-withdraw only for an NLRI that parsed. The FlowSpec plugin's NLRI syntax rule
# (judgeFlowSpecSection, internal/component/bgp/plugins/nlri/flowspec/rfc8955_syntax.go)
# runs from message.validateMPNLRISyntax on every MP_REACH_NLRI section, before any plugin
# decodes it.
#
# Two UPDATEs, in TCP order on one session, each an AFI 2 / SAFI 133 MP_REACH_NLRI with no
# next hop, ORIGIN IGP, an empty AS_PATH and LOCAL_PREF 100. Both carry the NLRI of the
# ExaBGP 4.2 corpus (qa/old responses-4.2): destination 2a02:b80:15:0:7aca:39ff:feae:a87a/128,
# source-ipv6 ::/128 at offset 120, next-header 17, destination-port 25:
#   U1: the well-formed twin, with the one pattern octet RFC 8956 Section 3.1 asks for.
#       The session stays up.
#   U2: the corpus bytes, sixteen pattern octets where one belongs: the fifteen extra
#       octets read as a component of type 0, unknown, so ze resets the session with
#       NOTIFICATION 3/9 (RFC 4760 Section 7), its Data the whole MP_REACH_NLRI attribute
#       (RFC 4271 Section 6.3). It is the fence: ze reads one connection on one goroutine in TCP
#       order, so the reset naming U2's own defect proves U1 was judged well-formed and the
#       session survived it. Had U1 reset the session, U2 would never be read.

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=15s
option=linger:value=true

# End-of-RIB for ipv6/flow: an MP_UNREACH_NLRI (extended length) holding AFI 2 / SAFI 133 alone.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF001E0200000007900F0003000285

action=send:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF004F02000000384001010040020040050400000064800E270002850000210180002A020B80001500007ACA39FFFEAEA87A028078010381110B81650D9107DD
action=send:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF005E02000000474001010040020040050400000064800E360002850000300180002A020B80001500007ACA39FFFEAEA87A028078000000000000000000000000000000010381110B81650D9107DD

# NOTIFICATION code 3 (UPDATE Message Error), subcode 9 (Optional Attribute Error), for U2,
# Data = U2's MP_REACH_NLRI attribute: flags 0x80, type 14, length 0x36 and its value.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=3:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF004E030309800E360002850000300180002A020B80001500007ACA39FFFEAEA87A028078000000000000000000000000000000010381110B81650D9107DD
EOF_PEER

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv6/flow { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=15s
# U2's own defect, the unknown component type, names the reset: U1 did not cause it.
expect=stderr:contains=RFC 8955 Section 4.2: malformed ipv6/flow NLRI: flowspec: invalid component type
reject=stderr:pattern=panic
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
